Microsoft Learn: Introduction to Azure Virtual Machines

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to Azure Virtual Machines
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how Azure Virtual Machines (VMs) work, how to plan and deploy them, the management options available, and how to ensure their availability and protection through backup and resiliency features.

This module focused on the fundamentals of Azure Virtual Machines, including deployment planning, VM creation methods, availability options, management tools, and backup strategies used in enterprise cloud environments.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Virtual Machines

The module introduced Azure Virtual Machines as Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) resources that provide on-demand computing power in the cloud.

Azure Virtual Machines allow organizations to run:

Windows Server
Linux
Custom operating systems
Enterprise applications
Databases
Development environments
Benefits:
Full operating system control
Rapid deployment
Scalability
Pay-as-you-go pricing
Hybrid cloud support
Example:
Azure Datacenter
       ↓
Virtual Machine
       ↓
Windows Server 2022

The module emphasized that Azure VMs are one of the most commonly used Azure services.

Step 2: Compiling a Checklist Before Creating a VM

A major focus of the module was planning before deployment.

Important decisions:
Category	Examples
Operating System	Windows or Linux
VM Size	CPU, Memory, Storage
Region	East US, West US, etc.
Networking	VNet, Subnet, Public IP
Storage	Standard or Premium disks
Availability	Availability Set or Availability Zone
Security	NSGs, Identity, RBAC
Key lessons learned:
Proper planning reduces future migration work
VM sizing directly impacts performance and cost
Networking and security should be designed before deployment

The module highlighted planning as a critical administrator responsibility.

